+## v0.28.0: Correct the online Unlimited NOS clock
+
+Unlimited NOS now refreshes an already-active NOS timestamp using the same clock the native game selected. The previous version always used the world clock; in an online fixture where the game's real clock was ahead, that made a full kit immediately appear depleted and caused repeated native stop/restart cycles. The corrected code retains the burst in that exact native test.
+
+This is a reproduced timing defect, **not proof of the clock values in your live run or a guarantee that NOS never stops**. Native AI can still cancel NOS for normal driving decisions. This update does not hold Alt, force activation, suppress native stops or change driving, shifting, steering or lane choice. Inactive and sentinel timestamps remain inactive; network permission and current-car/kit checks remain mandatory.
+
+For an online comparison, use the same ordinary driving situation with **Unlimited NOS** and **Allow network tools** enabled. If tapping remains, use **Export crash log** immediately. The new log samples the selected clock, active-since stamp and kit/resource accounting **before refill**; it contains no pointers. The timestamp flag does not prove successful activation, and sampled logs can miss short transitions.
+
+The final combined suite passes **1,740 tests, zero failures/skips/cancellations**. Saved tool profiles, working Street X behavior, reviewed Drift isolation, Drag protection, online collision/corner settings and the full tuner with snapshot diagnostics remain together. No native game code or profile was changed. The separate underlying tuner snapshot rejection still requires its S-code/log; this update does not claim to fix it. Live NOS behavior and game-save persistence remain unverified.
+
+## Retained v0.27.0: Identify rejected tuner snapshots
 
 This update replaces the generic “Full tuner snapshot layout is unsupported” message with a specific **S-code and component** when the native capture guard rejects a snapshot. The local diagnostic export records only the bounded failure code and applicable counts, never raw pointers or a memory dump. Details are reset for each request so a previous failure is not shown as a new one.
 
